{"schema":"cr/v1","tip":"f28e8ee3813e2a611a2a9dfc0ef386d89221adba","base":"99f72e822b71a338886d29375ecd36f2b49a62e0","branch":"wu/E-SEED3/w5-clone-setup","committer":"vuluu <quangvu.luu92@gmail.com>","timestamp":1794653432,"verdict":"FAIL","blockers":1,"importants":1,"code_diff_hash":"2f53dcafa7d5c10e0c56e4e74849f82ce670c5da","round":2,"scope":"full","findings":[{"location":"src/init.rs:275","claim":"A clone that ships .keel/skills.lock as a symlink to a file outside the repository gets that target silently overwritten with the lock text by a plain `keel init` (exit 0, no prompt, no -f): the read_to_string at :273 follows the link so the unreadable guard at :275 is false, and std::fs::write at :332 writes through it while printing `updated .keel/skills.lock`, although the same module keeps a symlinked .agents and .agents/skills as found and never follows them.","severity":"blocker","evidence":"src/init.rs:273 read_to_string follows the link, :275 marks unreadable only when the read fails, :330-333 writes through it labelled updated; src/init.rs:68-79,93-110 keep symlinked .agents as found (tests/init.rs:165-181); reproduced at the tip with target/debug/keel - a lock symlinked to an out-of-repo target was replaced with LOCK_HEADER plus hash lines, exit 0"},{"location":"src/init.rs:332","claim":"A .keel/skills.lock that is readable but not writable, when its body must change (any upgrade, or a stale lock missing entries), makes `keel init` exit 1 at the lock write before project-router, shared_skills, settings, .codex/hooks.json and hooks_install (src/init.rs:418-427), leaving the clone half-wired, whereas the adjacent unreadable-lock case is deliberately kept and init finishes.","severity":"important","evidence":"src/init.rs:273-275 the kept flag covers only an unreadable lock, :326-327 the kept message only there, :330-333 the write with `?`, :412-427 the order; reproduced with target/debug/keel - chmod 444 on the lock after dropping one entry gives `keel: write .keel/skills.lock: Permission denied`, exit 1, .claude/settings.json and .codex/hooks.json absent and core.hooksPath unset; tests/init.rs:854-879 covers only the unreadable paths"}],"refuted":[{"location":"src/init.rs:264","claim":"The lock records only content hashes and no build version, so skill_action cannot tell an upgrade from a downgrade: an older keel than the repo's committed skills takes the Upgrade branch and silently replaces those files and the lock with the older build's copy without asking.","why":"src/init.rs:260-266 any hash match takes Upgrade, direction-agnostic, and :330-333 rewrites the lock; but docs/work/2026-11-13-e-seed3-w5-clone-setup-plan.html:40,51,55 deliberately chose the hash-only rule (safe by construction: a file is replaced silently only when its bytes are a version Keel itself wrote) with the owner go at :12, every update is reported and the files are git-recoverable, and tests/init.rs:735-766 pin the same mechanism, so it is the approved rule, not a wrong result"},{"location":"src/init.rs:234","claim":"The lock per-file hash is made by stripping the sha256: prefix from crate::notes::sha256, coupling the init lock to the notes evidence format rather than a shared hash primitive, so a change to how notes names or formats hashes changes every lock value.","why":"src/notes.rs:179-183 is the tree shared sha256 primitive, used by src/mr.rs:4,329,345,350 too, not a notes-only serializer; src/init.rs:233-235 strips the prefix to the lock shasum form in one line and src/init.rs:559-561 pins the derived 64-hex value, so a format change fails a test instead of drifting a rule; only a module-placement preference remains"},{"location":"src/init.rs:447","claim":"The rule that decides whether the keel on PATH really is Keel is implemented twice (is_keel starts_with(KEEL_MARK) and keel_guard! grep -q ^<mark>), and main.rs test re-spells the shell predicate by hand, so the trigger condition must be kept in step by hand across init.rs and main.rs.","why":"the drift-prone datum is single-sourced: src/main.rs:351-363 defines keel_mark! and KEEL_MARK, :368-372 builds the grep from keel_mark!(), src/init.rs:7,447-448 uses crate::KEEL_MARK, and src/main.rs:713-715 interpolates it, while src/main.rs:693-703 and src/init.rs:576-580 pin the ^ anchor on the same samples; a shell-side check before exec keel (src/main.rs:369-381) is inherent, not a second rule"},{"location":"src/init.rs:439","claim":"The compatibility cutoff a Keel older than 2026-11-10 and the clash wording are restated from the hook-shim message at src/main.rs:383 with no shared constant and no test pinning either date, so a future marker/cutoff change leaves one of the two user-facing statements stale.","why":"the shim copy is pinned: src/main.rs:698 spells 2026-11-10 inside the exact-line assertion at :693-703; the texts are not verbatim (src/init.rs:439 adds the marker/refuse clauses, src/main.rs:383 adds put Keel first on PATH), the date is also user prose in docs/install.html:24 so a Rust const cannot be its one source, and tests/init.rs:820-847 ties the warning to the shim actual refusal"}],"nits":[],"fleet":{"dimensions":["correctness","efficiency","altitude"],"finders":3,"verifiers":3,"verifier_model":"deepseek-flash","candidates":{"blocker":1,"important":3,"nit":2},"by_finder":{"correctness":{"found":3,"verified":2},"efficiency":{"found":0,"verified":0},"altitude":{"found":3,"verified":0}},"waivers":[]}}
